WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:01.720
RYAN MATSUMOTO:
Cloud computing has

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:03.970
helped many industries
innovate to new heights,

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.470
and healthcare is no exception.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.188
In previous videos
in this series,

00:00:08.188 --> 00:00:09.730
we looked at how
the Cloud Healthcare

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.740
API can help you store
and access healthcare

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.840
data in Google Cloud.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.300
In this episode, we'll explore
how the Cloud Healthcare

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.270
API can be used to store,
retrieve, and analyze

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.440
medical imaging data.

00:00:26.107 --> 00:00:28.690
There are several key challenges
that healthcare professionals

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.120
face when dealing
with medical imaging.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.370
First, you need to
ensure HIPAA compliance

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.460
in clinical workflows to
ensure patient privacy.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.530
Second, researchers
often have to learn

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:42.310
about new technologies, which
can be confusing and expensive.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.470
And third, it can be
difficult to leverage

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.540
this data to gain
important insights using

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.390
big data and machine learning.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.340
Luckily, the Cloud
Healthcare API

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.740
addresses these challenges
with one of its endpoints

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.630
and comes packed with
other helpful features

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.090
for medical imaging analysis.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.330
It supports Digital Imaging
and Communications In Medicine,

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.480
also known as DICOM, an
international standard file

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.330
format used for storing and
transmitting medical images

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.160
across technologies.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.690
This could include x-rays,
MRIs, ultrasounds, and more.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.240
It can also help you save
money by enhancing or even

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.060
removing the need for
specific on-premise software

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.700
that requires expensive
licensing fees.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.160
And it makes it easy to
scale customer architectures,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.730
while maintaining low
latency and high performance.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.370
The Cloud Healthcare
API also helps

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.120
you leverage the power
of machine learning

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.750
by integrating well with Vertex
AI, Google Cloud's unified AI

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.140
platform.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.130
And finally, it connects
easily with open-source tools,

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.340
like the Open Health Imaging
Foundation Viewer, also known

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.130
as the OHIF Viewer, which
lets you view medical images

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.140
for the purpose of analysis.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.640
This is because the
Cloud Healthcare

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.872
API exposes the DICOM store
through a DICOM web interface.

00:01:52.872 --> 00:01:55.330
Users who might be interested
in using the Cloud Healthcare

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.030
API for imaging
include radiologists

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.510
who may want to view images,
researchers and data scientists

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.300
who may want to use
images for diagnostics,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.510
and IT decision-makers in
clinical organizations who

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.120
are looking to reduce costs
and improve storage, scale,

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.912
and elasticity.

00:02:12.912 --> 00:02:15.370
Let's take a look at an example
of how the Cloud Healthcare

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.160
API can be used to build a
spine detection machine learning

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.820
model using a small
set of DICOM CT images.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.240
First, images are ingested
into a DICOM store.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.160
A data store is simply
a place to store

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.840
a certain type of
healthcare data,

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.280
so a DICOM store is a place
to store DICOM medical images.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.980
Next, we can view the images
from the DICOM store using

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.490
OHIF, an open-source medical
imaging and viewing tool that

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.850
integrates directly with the
Google Cloud Healthcare API.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.070
Images can then be
parsed into metadata

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:48.070
and streamed to BigQuery
for further analysis.

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.950
BigQuery is Google Cloud's
large-scale data warehouse

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.990
that's great for
storing, analyzing,

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.300
and visualizing large datasets.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.400
With metadata ingested
into BigQuery,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.010
it becomes much easier to
search across a large amount

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.290
of image metadata that
wouldn't be readily

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.550
searchable in other systems.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.510
For example, we could search
for the most recent 20 images

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.730
of lung cancer diagnosis.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.660
Once our BigQuery
search is done,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.930
we can use the
corresponding DICOM web path

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.450
to find the specific image
for further analysis.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.830
The next step is to use filtered
export to export specific image

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.020
instances to Cloud
Storage, which

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.780
is used to store file
objects in the Cloud.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.400
Filtered export is
useful because you

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.800
may want to export specific
images from a larger

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.210
dataset to Cloud Storage and
convert them from DICOM to PNG

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.330
or JPEG for further analysis.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.220
Once the images are
in Cloud Storage,

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.960
we can then start
training our machine

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.290
learning model using these
images as our test dataset.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.010
First, we can import the images
into Vertex AI as an object

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:49.720
detection dataset.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.210
Vertex AI is Google
Cloud's unified machine

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:55.180
learning platform that makes it
easy to build and train machine

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.610
learning models on Google Cloud.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.010
Then we can label these
test images directly

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.590
in Vertex AI using
the Cloud Console.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.830
Here's where we can label images
that have a spine in them.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.600
Once our test
dataset is ready, we

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.590
can start to train
our prediction model.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.620
We can use AutoML,
which actually

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.580
does most of the work for us.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.360
All we have to do is provide
a label training dataset,

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.279
and Google Cloud
automatically builds

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.140
us a machine learning
model that leverages

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.570
its powerful
computing resources.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.660
No prior knowledge of
machine learning is required.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.415
Once AutoML finishes
building the ML model,

00:04:28.415 --> 00:04:30.040
we'll get an online
prediction endpoint

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.790
that can be used to
predict whether or not

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.840
new images have spines in them.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.600
The final step is to use the
OHIF viewer to view images

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.820
from the Healthcare
API DICOM store

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.790
and use the online prediction
endpoint to give us

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.830
our ML predictions.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:48.070
Here we have our image being
shown in the OHIF viewer.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.800
Under Predictions, we
can click Find Spine

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.670
to call the online prediction
endpoint hosted on Vertex AI.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.880
And since a spine is detected,
it is outlined with a box

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.478
directly in the
image viewer itself.

00:05:00.478 --> 00:05:02.520
We can also take a look
at the JavaScript Console

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.187
to see what's going
on behind the scenes

00:05:04.187 --> 00:05:06.180
when we hit Find Spine.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.830
There are just two API calls.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.920
The first API call is a render
request to the Cloud Healthcare

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.130
API, which we use to get a
rendered view of the image.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.400
The second API call is
to an online prediction

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.665
endpoint hosted on Vertex AI.

00:05:19.665 --> 00:05:21.540
This is what we use to
predict whether or not

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.700
there's a spine in the image.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.460
The response we get back
includes confidence scores,

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.260
as well as bounding
box information

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.300
for the spine detected.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.790
In the end, OHIF renders
the image as well as

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.520
the bounding box on
top of the image.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.770
As you can see, the
Cloud Healthcare API

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.660
provides a powerful
platform to help you

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.970
analyze medical imaging data.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.250
An important feature is
that it integrates well

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.250
with Google Cloud products
like BigQuery, Cloud Storage,

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.560
and Vertex AI,
giving you new ways

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.620
to gain invaluable insights
about medical imaging data

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.280
while complying with HIPPA and
other government regulations.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.390
To learn more, visit
cloud.google.com/healthcare.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:03.520
To get started, you'll need to
have a Google Cloud project.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.700
If you don't have one,
we've included a link

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.350
to a trial account
with free credits

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:11.370
in this video subscription along
with other helpful resources.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.960
And friends, if you found
this episode helpful,

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.230
please subscribe to the
channel to get notifications

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.860
of more healthcare episodes.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:18.360
Cheers.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

